### Set PORT in backend/.env to match (main.ts falls back to 3000).
### NOTE: the app has no setGlobalPrefix, and controllers are @Controller('auth'),
### @Controller('posts'), ... — so paths are /auth/..., NOT /api/auth/...
@host = http://localhost:4000

### =====================================================================
### Auth & Membership
### =====================================================================

### A1. Request OTP
POST {{host}}/auth/register/request-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "password": "test1234"
}

### A2. Verify OTP (เอา OTP จาก terminal backend log มาแทน)
POST {{host}}/auth/register/verify-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "otp": "000000"
}

### A3. Login (ใช้ email/password ที่สมัครผ่าน OTP flow ข้างบนแล้วเท่านั้น)
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "password": "test1234"
}

### A4. Login ผิดรหัสผ่าน -> expect 401
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test.new@ku.th",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### =====================================================================
### §7.7 Report & Moderation
### =====================================================================
###
### Runs against the mock data seeded by init.sql (docker compose up).
### Users:  1 = admin (isAdmin), 2-12 = regular users
### Seeded reports (only #1 and #2 are used below; #3-#5 are already decided
### or unrelated, so they are left alone):
###   #1  Transaction / target_id 1 / reporter 3 / Under_Investigation / handled_by 1
###       └─ Transaction 1: buyer 3, seller 2  → reported party = seller 2
###       └─ already has Appeal #1 (by user 2, Pending)
###   #2  Post / target_id 5 / reporter 6 / Pending
###       └─ Post 5 → Item 5 → owner 5        → reported party = user 5
###   #3  User / target_id 11 / Violation_Confirmed  → already has Appeal #2 (Rejected)
###   #4  Post / target_id 1  / Report_Dismissed
###   #5  User / target_id 9  / Pending
###
### The reported party is resolved by joining the real tables (see
### AppealService.resolveReportedUserId), so these ids come from the DB.
###
### Request bodies use snake_case (those are the DTO field names). Responses
### come straight from Prisma, whose model fields are camelCase — hence
### `$.reportId` / `$.appealId` in the chained references below.

### 1. POST /reports — no auth header -> expect 401
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "report_type": "User",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam"
}

### 2. POST /reports — with x-user-id -> expect 201 + status: Pending
# @name createReport
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "User",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam",
  "details": "Sending unsolicited messages in chat",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/evidence1.jpg"]
}

### 3. POST /reports — invalid report_type -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/reports
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 2

{
  "report_type": "NotARealType",
  "target_id": 4,
  "reason_category": "Spam"
}

### 4. GET /admin/reports — no admin header -> expect 403
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 2

### 5. GET /admin/reports — as admin -> expect 200 + list
GET {{host}}/admin/reports
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

### 6. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — resolve the report from #2 -> expect 200
### run #2 first so the id resolves
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed",
  "resolution_note": "Confirmed after reviewing chat history."
}

### 7. POST /admin/reports/:id/resolve — resolve the SAME report again -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/{{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}}/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Report_Dismissed"
}

### 8. POST /admin/reports/999/resolve — nonexistent report -> expect 404
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/999/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### === Appeals — reported party resolved by real DB join ===

### 9. POST /appeals — wrong user appeals the report from #2 -> expect 403
### #2 is report_type User with target_id 4, so only user 4 may appeal it.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 7

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Not my report"
}

### 10. POST /appeals — the reported user (4) appeals -> expect 201, status Pending
# @name createAppeal
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Those messages were about a legitimate transaction.",
  "evidence_urls": ["https://example.com/appeal-evidence.jpg"]
}

### 11. POST /appeals — appeal the same report twice -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": {{createReport.response.body.$.reportId}},
  "reason": "Trying again"
}

### 12. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — same admin who resolved the report (1) -> expect 400
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved"
}

### 13. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — a different admin -> expect 200
### Approving also flips the report to Report_Dismissed (verify with #5).
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 9
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Approved",
  "resolution_note": "Reviewed chat log, messages were transaction-related."
}

### 14. POST /admin/appeals/:id/decide — decide the SAME appeal again -> expect 400
### Without this guard the appeal would flip to Rejected while its report stayed
### Report_Dismissed, and the original handledBy/handledAt would be overwritten.
POST {{host}}/admin/appeals/{{createAppeal.response.body.$.appealId}}/decide
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 10
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Rejected",
  "resolution_note": "Attempt to overwrite an already-approved appeal"
}

### === Reported-party resolution for Post / Transaction reports ===
### These use the seeded reports, so run them on a fresh `docker compose up`
### (report #2 must still be unresolved for the appeal window check to pass).

### 15. POST /admin/reports/2/resolve — resolve the seeded Post report -> expect 200
POST {{host}}/admin/reports/2/resolve
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 1
x-is-admin: true

{
  "status": "Violation_Confirmed"
}

### 16. POST /appeals — wrong user appeals report 2 -> expect 403
### Report 2 targets Post 5, whose Item is owned by user 5 — user 4 is unrelated.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 4

{
  "report_id": 2,
  "reason": "Not my post"
}

### 17. POST /appeals — the real Post owner (user 5) appeals -> expect 201
### Resolved via Post 5 -> Item 5 -> ownerId 5.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 5

{
  "report_id": 2,
  "reason": "The description matched the actual condition."
}

### 18. POST /appeals — reporter of the Transaction report tries to appeal -> expect 403
### Report 1 targets Transaction 1 (buyer 3, seller 2) and was filed by user 3,
### so the reported party is the counterparty (seller 2), not the reporter.
POST {{host}}/appeals
Content-Type: application/json
x-user-id: 3

{
  "report_id": 1,
  "reason": "I filed this report myself"
}
